   "text": {
    "L1": "Numbers become larger as we move left through place values.",
    "L2": "A comma is not decorative. In the Indian system, its position follows a fixed pattern.",
    "L3": "Each place is ten times the place to its right: ones, tens, hundreds, thousands and lakhs.",
    "L4": "To write one lakh seven thousand forty, place the digits as 1, 0, 7, 0, 4, 0.",
    "L5": "The zeros hold empty places, so the number is 1,07,040, not a shorter number.",
    "L6": "First put a comma after three digits from the right, then after every two digits.",
    "L7": "A number with more digits is bigger; 1,00,000 is the smallest six-digit number and is ten times 10,000.",
    "L8": "Now choose the correct Indian comma pattern for 125000."
   },
